event: assistant.delta
data: {"message_id": "msg_536082e0c09b4e3a82e31485f865ecc7", "delta": ".", "session_id": "eccb47f1-d8bd-46a4-b78b-d0cd6970f2d8", "run_id": "run_c589b5b0b9414dfabc58adb8419f0d94", "seq": 878, "ts": 1795521649.783916}

event: assistant.completed
data: {"session_id": "eccb47f1-d8bd-46a4-b78b-d0cd6970f2d8", "message_id": "msg_536082e0c09b4e3a82e31485f865ecc7", "content": "When the storm found the Maribel, the Pacific had been empty for eleven days. Elias Venn had begun to think of the ocean as a room with no walls: blue by day, black by night, and always breathing beneath the little schooner. He was wrong. At dusk, the western horizon climbed toward him, a dark range of cloud lit from within by lightning.\n\nHe reefed the mainsail before the first gust struck. The wind hit like a door slammed by a giant, flattening the sea and snapping the canvas taut. Elias tied himself to the mast, then hauled down the last strip of sail. Rain needled his face. Beyond the rail, waves rose in long, green backs, each crowned with torn white foam.\n\nThe Maribel climbed the first wall, shuddered at the crest, and dropped into a trough so deep Elias saw nothing but water. The next wave swept over the deck. It took the spare line, a bucket, and the little brass compass his father had given him. Elias watched the compass vanish, then tightened his grip on the tiller. Grief could wait. The boat could not.\n\nHe steered by the wind and the feel of the hull. The rudder bucked in his hands. Lightning opened the sky, revealing wave after wave, a whole mountain range moving beneath him. Between flashes, darkness swallowed the mast. He spoke to the Maribel, not because he believed she could hear, but because silence made the storm seem larger.\n\n“Hold together,” he told her. “Just until morning.”\n\nA crack answered from below. Elias crawled forward, clipped to the safety line, and found water sloshing ankle-deep in the cabin. A seam near the bow had opened. He wedged his shoulder against the door while another wave battered the hull, then reached for the pump. The handle jolted like a living thing. He worked it until his arms trembled, pausing only to shovel water from the floor with a cooking pot.\n\nThe storm dragged on. Hours lost their edges. Once, the tiller tore free and slammed across the deck, pinning Elias’s sleeve. He cut the cloth with his knife and caught the helm before the schooner turned broadside. Once, a wave lifted the stern so high he felt the ocean tugging the boat backward, down into its dark throat. He shut his eyes, breathed once, and let the bow fall with the water.\n\nNear midnight, the radio gave a burst of static. Elias called the coast station, repeating the Maribel’s name and position until his voice rasped away. No answer came. He tried again, and again. On the fourth call, a faint voice returned—broken, distant, but human. They had heard him. They told him to keep east and promised to alert a ship in the area.\n\nHe kept east. The waves grew confused, colliding from different directions. The Maribel rolled until the mast nearly touched the sea. A cabinet tore loose below and crashed against the hull. Elias lashed himself to the wheel and held on, counting each breath. He imagined the shoreline not as a place, but as a single small light that had not yet appeared.\n\nAt the darkest hour, the wind began to soften. The change was so slight he mistook it for exhaustion. Then the rain thinned. A pale seam showed beneath the clouds, and the sea’s black ridges turned iron gray. Elias looked east and saw a ship’s lights, two steady stars riding low above the water.\n\nThe rescue vessel approached carefully, its horn sounding through the last gusts. Sailors tossed a line. Elias caught it on the second try, and strong hands drew him aboard. He glanced back at the Maribel, battered, leaking, still afloat. Dawn touched her wet deck with gold.\n\nOn the rescue ship, someone wrapped a blanket around his shoulders and offered him coffee. Elias held the cup in both hands. The Pacific stretched behind them, vast and restless, but no longer empty. He watched the Maribel rise on a final swell, her patched sail fluttering like a small, stubborn flag, and understood that crossing the ocean had never meant conquering it. It meant listening, enduring, and knowing when to ask the horizon for help.", "completed": true, "partial": false, "interrupted": false, "runtime": {"provider": "", "model": "", "route_source": "global", "requested": {"provider": "", "model": ""}}, "run_id": "run_c589b5b0b9414dfabc58adb8419f0d94", "seq": 879, "ts": 1795521650.1992261}

event: run.completed
data: {"session_id": "eccb47f1-d8bd-46a4-b78b-d0cd6970f2d8", "message_id": "msg_536082e0c09b4e3a82e31485f865ecc7", "completed": true, "partial": false, "interrupted": false, "messages": [{"role": "assistant", "content": "", "tool_calls": [{"call_id": "call_lYyypuSaSIZes1xObwKc6Vki", "function": {"arguments": "{\"code\":\"story = \\\"\\\"\\\"When the storm found the Maribel, the Pacific had been empty for eleven days. Elias Venn had begun to think of the ocean as a room with no walls: blue by day, black by night, and always breathing beneath the little schooner. He was wrong. At dusk, the western horizon climbed toward him, a dark range of cloud lit from within by silent lightning.\\n\\nHe reefed the mainsail before the first gust struck. The wind hit like a door slammed by a giant, flattening the sea and snapping the canvas taut. Elias tied himself to the mast, then hauled down the last strip of sail. Rain needled his face. Beyond the rail, waves rose in long, green backs, each crowned with torn white foam.\\n\\nThe Maribel climbed the first wall, shuddered at the crest, and dropped into a trough so deep Elias saw nothing but water. The next wave swept over the deck. It took the spare line, a bucket, and the little brass compass his father had given him. Elias watched the compass vanish, then tightened his grip on the tiller. Grief could wait. The boat could not.\\n\\nHe steered by the wind and the feel of the hull. The rudder bucked in his hands. Lightning opened the sky, revealing wave after wave, a whole mountain range moving beneath him. Between flashes, darkness swallowed the mast. He spoke aloud to the Maribel, not because he believed she could hear, but because silence made the storm seem larger.\\n\\n“Hold together,” he told her. “Just until morning.”\\n\\nA crack answered from below. Elias crawled forward, clipped to the safety line, and found water sloshing ankle-deep in the cabin. A seam near the bow had opened. He wedged his shoulder against the door while another wave battered the hull, then reached for the pump. The handle jolted like a living thing. He worked it until his arms trembled, pausing only to shovel water from the floor with a cooking pot.\\n\\nThe storm dragged on. Hours lost their edges. Once, the tiller tore free and slammed across the deck, pinning Elias’s sleeve. He cut the cloth with his knife and caught the helm before the schooner turned broadside. Once, a wave lifted the stern so high he felt the ocean tugging the boat backward, down into its dark throat. He shut his eyes, breathed once, and let the bow fall with the water.\\n\\nNear midnight, the radio gave a burst of static. Elias called the coast station, repeating the Maribel’s name and position until his voice rasped away. No answer came. He tried again, and again. On the fourth call, a faint voice returned—broken, distant, but human. They had heard him. They told him to keep east and promised to alert a ship in the area.\\n\\nElias laughed, though no one could hear it. Then the radio went dead.\\n\\nHe kept east. The waves grew confused, colliding from different directions. The Maribel rolled until the mast nearly touched the sea. A cabinet tore loose below and crashed against the hull. Elias lashed himself to the wheel and held on, counting each breath. He imagined the shoreline not as a place, but as a single small light that had not yet appeared.\\n\\nAt the darkest hour, the wind began to soften. The change was so slight he mistook it for exhaustion. Then the rain thinned. A pale seam showed beneath the clouds, and the sea’s black ridges turned iron gray. Elias looked east and saw a ship’s lights, two steady stars riding low above the water.\\n\\nThe rescue vessel approached carefully, its horn sounding through the last gusts. Sailors tossed a line. Elias caught it on the second try, and strong hands drew him aboard. He glanced back at the Maribel, battered, leaking, still afloat. Dawn touched her wet deck with gold.\\n\\nOn the rescue ship, someone wrapped a blanket around his shoulders and offered him coffee. Elias held the cup in both hands. The Pacific stretched behind them, vast and restless, but no longer empty. He watched the Maribel rise on a final swell, her patched sail fluttering like a small, stubborn flag, and understood that crossing the ocean had never meant conquering it. It meant listening, enduring, and knowing when to ask the horizon for help.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\nprint(len(story.split()))\"}", "name": "execute_code"}, "id": "call_lYyypuSaSIZes1xObwKc6Vki", "response_item_id": "fc_00183e50063b7b80016b057c2c2d3487d0ba2b95ce599dc434", "type": "function"}], "timestamp": 1795521596.170378, "finish_reason": "tool_calls", "reasoning": null}, {"role": "tool", "content": "{\"status\": \"error\", \"error\": \"BLOCKED: execute_code runs arbitrary local Python (including subprocess calls that bypass shell-string approval checks). This session runs on an unattended platform (api_server) with no user present to approve it. Use normal tools instead, or set approvals.unattended_mode: approve only if sessions on this surface are intentionally trusted.\", \"tool_calls_made\": 0, \"duration_seconds\": 0, \"user_summary\": \"This code is not allowed in an unattended session — it did not run.\"}", "tool_call_id": "call_lYyypuSaSIZes1xObwKc6Vki", "tool_name": "execute_code", "timestamp": 1795521596.194514}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "", "tool_calls": [{"call_id": "call_7Cvsdn7tHdYtUTixPUYCMSgQ", "function": {"arguments": "{\"command\":\"python3 -c \\\"text='''When the storm found the Maribel, the Pacific had been empty for eleven days. Elias Venn had begun to think of the ocean as a room with no walls: blue by day, black by night, and always breathing beneath the little schooner. He was wrong. At dusk, the western horizon climbed toward him, a dark range of cloud lit from within by silent lightning.\\\\n\\\\nHe reefed the mainsail before the first gust struck. The wind hit like a door slammed by a giant, flattening the sea and snapping the canvas taut. Elias tied himself to the mast, then hauled down the last strip of sail. Rain needled his face. Beyond the rail, waves rose in long, green backs, each crowned with torn white foam.\\\\n\\\\nThe Maribel climbed the first wall, shuddered at the crest, and dropped into a trough so deep Elias saw nothing but water. The next wave swept over the deck. It took the spare line, a bucket, and the little brass compass his father had given him. Elias watched the compass vanish, then tightened his grip on the tiller. Grief could wait. The boat could not.\\\\n\\\\nHe steered by the wind and the feel of the hull. The rudder bucked in his hands. Lightning opened the sky, revealing wave after wave, a whole mountain range moving beneath him. Between flashes, darkness swallowed the mast. He spoke aloud to the Maribel, not because he believed she could hear, but because silence made the storm seem larger.\\\\n\\\\n“Hold together,” he told her. “Just until morning.”\\\\n\\\\nA crack answered from below. Elias crawled forward, clipped to the safety line, and found water sloshing ankle-deep in the cabin. A seam near the bow had opened. He wedged his shoulder against the door while another wave battered the hull, then reached for the pump. The handle jolted like a living thing. He worked it until his arms trembled, pausing only to shovel water from the floor with a cooking pot.\\\\n\\\\nThe storm dragged on. Hours lost their edges. Once, the tiller tore free and slammed across the deck, pinning Elias’s sleeve. He cut the cloth with his knife and caught the helm before the schooner turned broadside. Once, a wave lifted the stern so high he felt the ocean tugging the boat backward, down into its dark throat. He shut his eyes, breathed once, and let the bow fall with the water.\\\\n\\\\nNear midnight, the radio gave a burst of static. Elias called the coast station, repeating the Maribel’s name and position until his voice rasped away. No answer came. He tried again, and again. On the fourth call, a faint voice returned—broken, distant, but human. They had heard him. They told him to keep east and promised to alert a ship in the area.\\\\n\\\\nElias laughed, though no one could hear it. Then the radio went dead.\\\\n\\\\nHe kept east. The waves grew confused, colliding from different directions. The Maribel rolled until the mast nearly touched the sea. A cabinet tore loose below and crashed against the hull. Elias lashed himself to the wheel and held on, counting each breath. He imagined the shoreline not as a place, but as a single small light that had not yet appeared.\\\\n\\\\nAt the darkest hour, the wind began to soften. The change was so slight he mistook it for exhaustion. Then the rain thinned. A pale seam showed beneath the clouds, and the sea’s black ridges turned iron gray. Elias looked east and saw a ship’s lights, two steady stars riding low above the water.\\\\n\\\\nThe rescue vessel approached carefully, its horn sounding through the last gusts. Sailors tossed a line. Elias caught it on the second try, and strong hands drew him aboard. He glanced back at the Maribel, battered, leaking, still afloat. Dawn touched her wet deck with gold.\\\\n\\\\nOn the rescue ship, someone wrapped a blanket around his shoulders and offered him coffee. Elias held the cup in both hands. The Pacific stretched behind them, vast and restless, but no longer empty. He watched the Maribel rise on a final swell, her patched sail fluttering like a small, stubborn flag, and understood that crossing the ocean had never meant conquering it. It meant listening, enduring, and knowing when to ask the horizon for help.'''; print(len(text.split()))\\\"\",\"timeout\":20}", "name": "terminal"}, "id": "call_7Cvsdn7tHdYtUTixPUYCMSgQ", "response_item_id": "fc_00183e50063b7b80016b057c4136ac87d08d9bac763b1e281b", "type": "function"}], "timestamp": 1795521615.871291, "finish_reason": "tool_calls", "reasoning": "**Counting story text**", "reasoning_content": "**Counting story text**"}, {"role": "tool", "content": "{\"output\": \"714\", \"exit_code\": 0, \"error\": null, \"approval\": \"Command was flagged (script execution via -e/-c flag) and auto-approved by smart approval.\"}", "tool_call_id": "call_7Cvsdn7tHdYtUTixPUYCMSgQ", "tool_name": "terminal", "timestamp": 1795521617.9117608}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "", "tool_calls": [{"call_id": "call_FSJewHXddpcCfaEFVk0vYjNm", "function": {"arguments": "{\"command\":\"python3 -c \\\"print(len('Elias laughed, though no one could hear it. Then the radio went dead.'.split()) + len('silent'.split()))\\\"\",\"timeout\":20}", "name": "terminal"}, "id": "call_FSJewHXddpcCfaEFVk0vYjNm", "response_item_id": "fc_00183e50063b7b80016b057c5d3f5c87d08f024e35cda25ad4", "type": "function"}], "timestamp": 1795521629.569889, "finish_reason": "tool_calls", "reasoning": "**Counting sentence tokens**", "reasoning_content": "**Counting sentence tokens**"}, {"role": "tool", "content": "{\"output\": \"14\", \"exit_code\": 0, \"error\": null, \"approval\": \"Command was flagged (script execution via -e/-c flag) and auto-approved by smart approval.\"}", "tool_call_id": "call_FSJewHXddpcCfaEFVk0vYjNm", "tool_name": "terminal", "timestamp": 1795521631.756516}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "When the storm found the Maribel, the Pacific had been empty for eleven days. Elias Venn had begun to think of the ocean as a room with no walls: blue by day, black by night, and always breathing beneath the little schooner. He was wrong. At dusk, the western horizon climbed toward him, a dark range of cloud lit from within by lightning.\n\nHe reefed the mainsail before the first gust struck. The wind hit like a door slammed by a giant, flattening the sea and snapping the canvas taut. Elias tied himself to the mast, then hauled down the last strip of sail. Rain needled his face. Beyond the rail, waves rose in long, green backs, each crowned with torn white foam.\n\nThe Maribel climbed the first wall, shuddered at the crest, and dropped into a trough so deep Elias saw nothing but water. The next wave swept over the deck. It took the spare line, a bucket, and the little brass compass his father had given him. Elias watched the compass vanish, then tightened his grip on the tiller. Grief could wait. The boat could not.\n\nHe steered by the wind and the feel of the hull. The rudder bucked in his hands. Lightning opened the sky, revealing wave after wave, a whole mountain range moving beneath him. Between flashes, darkness swallowed the mast. He spoke to the Maribel, not because he believed she could hear, but because silence made the storm seem larger.\n\n“Hold together,” he told her. “Just until morning.”\n\nA crack answered from below. Elias crawled forward, clipped to the safety line, and found water sloshing ankle-deep in the cabin. A seam near the bow had opened. He wedged his shoulder against the door while another wave battered the hull, then reached for the pump. The handle jolted like a living thing. He worked it until his arms trembled, pausing only to shovel water from the floor with a cooking pot.\n\nThe storm dragged on. Hours lost their edges. Once, the tiller tore free and slammed across the deck, pinning Elias’s sleeve. He cut the cloth with his knife and caught the helm before the schooner turned broadside. Once, a wave lifted the stern so high he felt the ocean tugging the boat backward, down into its dark throat. He shut his eyes, breathed once, and let the bow fall with the water.\n\nNear midnight, the radio gave a burst of static. Elias called the coast station, repeating the Maribel’s name and position until his voice rasped away. No answer came. He tried again, and again. On the fourth call, a faint voice returned—broken, distant, but human. They had heard him. They told him to keep east and promised to alert a ship in the area.\n\nHe kept east. The waves grew confused, colliding from different directions. The Maribel rolled until the mast nearly touched the sea. A cabinet tore loose below and crashed against the hull. Elias lashed himself to the wheel and held on, counting each breath. He imagined the shoreline not as a place, but as a single small light that had not yet appeared.\n\nAt the darkest hour, the wind began to soften. The change was so slight he mistook it for exhaustion. Then the rain thinned. A pale seam showed beneath the clouds, and the sea’s black ridges turned iron gray. Elias looked east and saw a ship’s lights, two steady stars riding low above the water.\n\nThe rescue vessel approached carefully, its horn sounding through the last gusts. Sailors tossed a line. Elias caught it on the second try, and strong hands drew him aboard. He glanced back at the Maribel, battered, leaking, still afloat. Dawn touched her wet deck with gold.\n\nOn the rescue ship, someone wrapped a blanket around his shoulders and offered him coffee. Elias held the cup in both hands. The Pacific stretched behind them, vast and restless, but no longer empty. He watched the Maribel rise on a final swell, her patched sail fluttering like a small, stubborn flag, and understood that crossing the ocean had never meant conquering it. It meant listening, enduring, and knowing when to ask the horizon for help.", "timestamp": 1795521650.107371, "finish_reason": "stop", "reasoning": null}], "usage": {"input_tokens": 72743, "output_tokens": 3332, "total_tokens": 76075}, "runtime": {"provider": "", "model": "", "route_source": "global", "requested": {"provider": "", "model": ""}}, "run_id": "run_c589b5b0b9414dfabc58adb8419f0d94", "seq": 880, "ts": 1795521650.199239}

